  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DEC Ukraine Humanitarian Appeal DPI Phase 2a</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Context: The scale of need in Ukraine has grown: OCHA suggest 16 million people need urgent assistance but estimate only two-thirds are receiving it. Alongside provision of emergency material assistance, there is a need to respond to other emerging issues such as trauma, accommodation and welfare entitlements. There is also a key need for winterisation support, as temperatures often reach -20C. Prior to war, dozens of people sleeping rough would freeze to death from hypothermia every year. The war has caused a sharp rise in homelessness (in its broad definition) so there is a risk this will be multiplied if support is not put in place. 

Programme overview: Depaul&apos;s project builds on the foundations and outcomes achieved during DEC Phase 1. The programme will sustain provision of emergency assistance and augment this with winterisation support, whilst also developing holistic support and accommodation services to meet the wider needs of affected populations. The duration is 12 months over phase 2a with activities anticipated to continue to phase 2b depending on need and availability of funding. Winterisation related activities will be predominantly delivered in the first six months although will continue as needed.

Key activities include:
Outcome A &#x2013; affected populations have access to food assistance
Outcome B &#x2013; affected populations have access to basic hygiene assistance
Outcome C &#x2013; Affected populations are supported with winterisation
Outcome D &#x2013; Affected populations have access to holistic support services (legal, MHPSS)
Outcome E &#x2013; Affected population have access to accommodation</narrative>
  </description>
